{
  "note": "Retinal Neurons and Lateral Inhibition",
  "source": "0e4a16f60e8a",
  "script": "359607f91fee",
  "voice": "Jessica Gallagher - Clear and Neutral",
  "model": "eleven_v4",
  "fingerprint": "a2d9c529df4c",
  "duration": 220.891,
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "Retinal Neurons and Lateral Inhibition",
      "start": 0,
      "sentences": [
        {
          "text": "The retina passes visual information through a short chain of neurones.",
          "start": 0,
          "end": 4.4
        },
        {
          "text": "Running across that chain are the horizontal and amacrine cells, and they mediate lateral inhibition in the retina.",
          "start": 4.64,
          "end": 11.44
        },
        {
          "text": "This retinal lateral inhibition enhances the quality of sensory information.",
          "start": 11.92,
          "end": 16.76
        },
        {
          "text": "It also provides the contrast information on which visual acuity depends.",
          "start": 16.96,
          "end": 21.72
        },
        {
          "text": "We'll follow the chain first, then see what lateral inhibition achieves there, and finally meet the two cell types that carry it out.",
          "start": 22,
          "end": 29.68
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "The retinal cell chain",
      "start": 30.537,
      "sentences": [
        {
          "text": "To begin, visual signals flow through the retina in a chain of three neurones.",
          "start": 30.537,
          "end": 36.19
        },
        {
          "text": "The rods and cones come first, and they signal to bipolar cells.",
          "start": 36.244,
          "end": 40.804
        },
        {
          "text": "The bipolar cells pass the signal on to ganglion cells.",
          "start": 40.83,
          "end": 44.724
        },
        {
          "text": "It is the axons of these ganglion cells that form the optic nerve.",
          "start": 44.75,
          "end": 49.177
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Lateral inhibition in the retina",
      "start": 49.946,
      "sentences": [
        {
          "text": "Next comes lateral inhibition itself.",
          "start": 49.946,
          "end": 52.986
        },
        {
          "text": "In general terms, it is a mechanism in which the most active pathway suppresses its flanking neighbours.",
          "start": 53.066,
          "end": 59.666
        },
        {
          "text": "For vision, though, what matters is what that mechanism achieves in the retina.",
          "start": 59.706,
          "end": 64.613
        },
        {
          "text": "For a start, lateral inhibition enhances the quality of sensory information.",
          "start": 64.639,
          "end": 69.986
        },
        {
          "text": "It also sharpens the difference between strongly and weakly stimulated pathways, and in doing so it provides contrast information.",
          "start": 70.026,
          "end": 78.466
        },
        {
          "text": "That contrast information, in turn, enables visual acuity.",
          "start": 78.506,
          "end": 83.026
        },
        {
          "text": "In other words, seeing a sharp image at all depends on this retinal contrast enhancement, and not just on the optics of the eye.",
          "start": 83.066,
          "end": 91.546
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Horizontal and amacrine cells mediate lateral inhibition",
      "start": 92.317,
      "sentences": [
        {
          "text": "So which cells carry out these lateral interactions?",
          "start": 92.317,
          "end": 95.97
        },
        {
          "text": "The answer is two further types of retinal neurone, which sit across the vertical chain.",
          "start": 96.024,
          "end": 101.89
        },
        {
          "text": "The first type is the horizontal cell.",
          "start": 101.944,
          "end": 104.517
        },
        {
          "text": "Horizontal cells lie at the level between the photoreceptors and the bipolar cells.",
          "start": 104.557,
          "end": 109.797
        },
        {
          "text": "Their processes mediate the lateral inhibition that maintains sensitivity to contrast.",
          "start": 109.837,
          "end": 115.557
        },
        {
          "text": "The second type is the amacrine cell, which lies one level deeper, between the bipolar cells and the ganglion cells.",
          "start": 115.597,
          "end": 123.077
        },
        {
          "text": "The processes of amacrine cells are postsynaptic to the bipolar cell terminals and presynaptic to the ganglion cell dendrites.",
          "start": 123.117,
          "end": 131.237
        },
        {
          "text": "Through these connections, they mediate lateral interactions at that deeper level as well.",
          "start": 131.277,
          "end": 136.757
        },
        {
          "text": "Together, the two cell types allow each point in the retinal image to be compared with its surround, before the signal ever leaves the eye.",
          "start": 136.797,
          "end": 145.237
        },
        {
          "text": "An example using the letter A makes this function concrete.",
          "start": 145.277,
          "end": 149.197
        },
        {
          "text": "After all, a letter is nothing more than dark strokes against a light background.",
          "start": 149.277,
          "end": 154.277
        },
        {
          "text": "Reading it therefore depends entirely on detecting the contrast at its edges.",
          "start": 154.317,
          "end": 159.277
        },
        {
          "text": "Lateral inhibition exaggerates exactly these edges, so the strokes of the letter A stand out crisply from the white around them.",
          "start": 159.317,
          "end": 167.317
        },
        {
          "text": "Thanks to this, letters can still be resolved as they shrink.",
          "start": 167.357,
          "end": 171.317
        },
        {
          "text": "Now, the smallest letter that can still be resolved defines visual acuity.",
          "start": 171.357,
          "end": 176.357
        },
        {
          "text": "It follows that acuity is a direct product of the contrast information that horizontal and amacrine cells create.",
          "start": 176.397,
          "end": 183.757
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Summary",
      "start": 184.529,
      "sentences": [
        {
          "text": "To sum up, the retinal signal travels from rods and cones to bipolar cells, and from there to ganglion cells.",
          "start": 184.529,
          "end": 192.342
        },
        {
          "text": "The axons of those ganglion cells form the optic nerve.",
          "start": 192.396,
          "end": 196.329
        },
        {
          "text": "Across this chain, horizontal and amacrine cells mediate lateral inhibition.",
          "start": 196.369,
          "end": 202.009
        },
        {
          "text": "That inhibition enhances the quality of sensory information and supplies contrast information.",
          "start": 202.049,
          "end": 208.289
        },
        {
          "text": "This is how it enables visual acuity, as illustrated by resolving the edges of the letter A.",
          "start": 208.369,
          "end": 214.569
        },
        {
          "text": "That completes our look at how retinal neurones sharpen contrast before the signal ever leaves the eye.",
          "start": 214.609,
          "end": 220.929
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
