    {"duration":92.212245,"alignment":{"characters":"So which cells carry out these lateral interactions? The answer is two further types of retinal neurone, which sit across the vertical chain. The first type is the horizontal cell. Horizontal cells lie at the level between the photoreceptors and the bipolar cells. Their processes mediate the lateral inhibition that maintains sensitivity to contrast. The second type is the amacrine cell, which lies one level deeper, between the bipolar cells and the ganglion cells. The processes of amacrine cells are postsynaptic to the bipolar cell terminals and presynaptic to the ganglion cell dendrites. Through these connections, they mediate lateral interactions at that deeper level as well. Together, the two cell types allow each point in the retinal image to be compared with its surround, before the signal ever leaves the eye. An example using the letter A makes this function concrete. After all, a letter is nothing more than dark strokes against a light background. Reading it therefore depends entirely on detecting the contrast at its edges. Lateral inhibition exaggerates exactly these edges, so the strokes of the letter A stand out crisply from the white around them. Thanks to this, letters can still be resolved as they shrink. Now, the smallest letter that can still be resolved defines visual acuity. It follows that acuity is a direct product of the contrast information that horizontal and amacrine cells create.","starts":[0,80,160,240,304,368,432,496,560,760,800,840,880,920,960,1040,1104,1168,1232,1296,1360,1440,1493,1547,1600,1720,1744,1768,1792,1816,1840,1880,1920,1973,2027,2080,2133,2187,2240,2320,2368,2416,2464,2512,2560,2709,2857,3006,3154,3303,3451,3600,3653,3707,3724,3742,3760,3840,3920,4000,4080,4160,4240,4320,4400,4480,4560,4640,4720,4800,4880,4960,5040,5080,5120,5160,5200,5240,5280,5480,5520,5560,5600,5640,5680,5760,5800,5840,5920,5947,5973,6000,6060,6120,6180,6240,6320,6360,6400,6480,6560,6693,6827,6960,7040,71