    {"duration":36.362449,"alignment":{"characters":"To sum up, the retinal signal travels from rods and cones to bipolar cells, and from there to ganglion cells. The axons of those ganglion cells form the optic nerve. Across this chain, horizontal and amacrine cells mediate lateral inhibition. That inhibition enhances the quality of sensory information and supplies contrast information. This is how it enables visual acuity, as illustrated by resolving the edges of the letter A. That completes our look at how retinal neurones sharpen contrast before the signal ever leaves the eye.","starts":[0,40,80,160,213,267,320,480,560,640,880,960,987,1013,1040,1120,1173,1227,1280,1320,1360,1400,1440,1600,1667,1733,1800,1867,1933,2000,2080,2120,2160,2200,2240,2320,2400,2480,2600,2630,2660,2690,2720,2800,2840,2880,2960,3040,3120,3147,3173,3200,3280,3360,3440,3520,3640,3760,3840,3920,4000,4040,4080,4200,4320,4380,4440,4500,4560,4640,4768,4896,5024,5152,5280,5333,5387,5404,5422,5440,5560,5590,5620,5650,5680,5920,5968,6016,6064,6112,6160,6240,6280,6320,6440,6470,6500,6530,6560,6620,6680,6740,6800,6880,7056,7232,7408,7584,7760,7813,7867,7884,7902,7920,8000,8120,8240,8320,8400,8480,8520,8540,8560,8640,8672,8704,8736,8768,8800,8920,8950,8980,9010,9040,9100,9160,9220,9280,9360,9440,9520,9600,9680,9760,9920,9980,10040,10100,10160,10280,10320,10360,10400,10600,10640,10680,10720,10760,10800,10880,10933,10987,11040,11400,11760,11800,11840,11907,11973,12040,12107,12173,12240,12320,12360,12400,12440,12480,12560,12672,12784,12896,13008,13120,13160,13200,13272,13344,13416,13488,13560,13632,13704,13776,13848,13920,14040,14080,14120,14160,14240,14300,14360,14420,14480,14540,14600,14660,14720,14960,15008,15056,15104,15152,15200,15280,15349,15417,15486,15554,15623,15691,15760,15800,15840,15893,15947,16000,16053,16107,16160,16240,16280,16320,16460,16600,16740,16880,17020,17160,17300,17440,17480,17520,17560,17600,17640,17680,17760,17800,17840,17910,17980,18050,18120,18190,18260,18330,18400,18560,1861